is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router_core_3
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Linux firewall (iptables/nftables) on router_core_3. Mechanism: all forwarded traffic with destination 10.200.0.0/24 is being dropped in the FORWARD chain, so packets from the pods (10.1.1.0/24 and 10.2.1.0/24) never reach the server farm. Localization: router_core_3, which is the transit router toward the server network via eth2 to switch_server_access (route shows 10.200.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.11 dev eth2). Evidence: on router_core_3, iptables -L -v -n shows “DROP … destination 10.200.0.0/24” in FORWARD with counters (27 packets, 2268 bytes), and nftables has the same rule: table ip filter, chain FORWARD: “ip daddr 10.200.0.0/24 … drop.” OSPF is healthy and routes are present (including 10.200.0.0/24), and ip_forward=1. Locally originated pings from router_core_3 to 10.200.0.3 succeed (OUTPUT policy ACCEPT), while host_1_1_1_1/host_2_1_1_1 to 10.200.0.x time out, matching the drop in the FORWARD path. The server segment itself is healthy (dns_server and web servers can ping each other). Root cause: a misconfigured firewall rule added on router_core_3 during the change window that drops all forwarded traffic destined to the 10.200.0.0/24 server subnet. Corrective fix: remove or adjust the rule to permit intended access, e.g., iptables -D FORWARD -d 10.200.0.0/24 -j DROP (and delete the equivalent nftables rule), then persist the corrected configuration. After removal, pod-to-server DNS/HTTP should restore immediately. 
=== END ===